{
  /*
   * THE LIBRARY BUILD.
   *
   * WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND WHY IT IS NOT `tsconfig.json`.
   * `tsconfig.json` is the TYPECHECK config: `noEmit: true`, and it includes
   * the application (`apps/wallet/`) and every test, because that is what
   * `npm run typecheck` has to read. A published package needs the opposite —
   * emitted JavaScript and declarations, and NOTHING from the application.
   * Making one file do both would either emit the app into the package or stop
   * the typecheck reading it. So the two configs are separate on purpose and
   * `tsconfig.json` is unchanged.
   *
   * WHAT IS IN THE LIBRARY, MEASURED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.
   * Every `.ts` under `src/` that is not a test, and not under `apps/wallet/`.
   * That is 38 files, and `src/test-setup.ts` is one of them: the exclude below
   * that names it is written from the repository root, so it resolves under
   * this directory to a path that does not exist and excludes nothing - the
   * library carries `lib/test-setup.js`. The measurement that makes the split
   * safe: NO file outside `apps/wallet/` imports anything from `apps/wallet/` or
   * through the `@/` alias — only three TEST files do, and tests are excluded
   * here and keep running from source under `vitest`. So the library half has
   * no edge into the application half and the exclusion cannot silently drop a
   * module the package needs.
   *
   * WHY THE EMITTED JAVASCRIPT RUNS UNDER A PLAIN NODE LOADER, WHICH IS THE
   * WHOLE POINT OF THIS FILE. `moduleResolution: Bundler` accepts extensionless
   * relative imports, and TypeScript emits import specifiers VERBATIM — so a
   * single extensionless relative import anywhere in this set would emit a
   * specifier Node ESM cannot resolve, and the package would fail exactly as it
   * does today, one layer further down. MEASURED: of the relative imports in
   * this set, zero are extensionless; every one already carries `.js`. The
   * extensionless spelling exists only under `apps/wallet/`, which shadcn's output
   * introduced and which is not in the package.
   *
   * `types` IS `["node"]` AND NOT THE BASE CONFIG'S LIST, FOR TWO SEPARATE
   * REASONS. What comes OUT: the base loads `vitest/globals` and `vite/client`,
   * a test runner's and a bundler's ambient types, which a consumer of this
   * package has not installed — a declaration file referring to them would not
   * typecheck on the other side. The one place the library reads
   * `import.meta.env` (`packages/identity/src/profile/origin.ts:89`) already does it through a
   * cast rather than through `vite/client`, so nothing here needs it.
   * What goes IN: `packages/identity/src/profile/disclosure.ts:101,192` uses the GLOBAL `Buffer`
   * with no import, so the build needs `@types/node` to name it. Emptying the
   * list made those two lines the only errors in the whole set, which is how
   * the dependency was found rather than assumed — and it is recorded as a
   * finding in its own right, because `@types/node` was not declared by this
   * package until the work that added this file declared it.
   */
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": false,
    "outDir": "./lib",
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "declaration": true,
    "declarationMap": true,
    "sourceMap": true,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": [
    "apps/wallet/**",
    "src/**/*.test.ts",
    "src/**/*.test.tsx",
    "packages/identity/src/test-setup.ts"
  ]
}
